  "/concrete-takeoff-calculator": "<h2>How a slab is built, step by step</h2><p>This page includes a step by step animated build sequence. The same steps are listed here so the method is readable without JavaScript.</p><ol><li><strong>Set out and level the area</strong>: Mark the slab outline and transfer the finished floor level from a single datum before any excavation starts. Check first: Diagonals equal and the level taken from one datum.</li><li><strong>Excavate and compact the subgrade</strong>: Strip topsoil, cut to formation level, then compact in layers until the surface is firm and uniform. Check first: No soft spots and no ponding after rain.</li><li><strong>Blind the surface</strong>: Lay and compact a thin blinding layer so the slab sits on a clean, level bed and any membrane is not punctured. Check first: Level within 10 mm and free of loose stones.</li><li><strong>Place the mesh or bars</strong>: Set the reinforcement on chairs so it finishes inside the slab, with laps tied and cover maintained. Check first: Cover maintained and chairs not walked out of place.</li><li><strong>Place and finish the concrete</strong>: Place the mixed concrete in one continuous run, screed to level, then trowel the finish the floor needs. Check first: No cold joints and curing started before it dries.</li><li><strong>Cure before loading</strong>: Keep the slab damp or covered for at least seven days and keep site traffic off until it has taken load. Check first: No cracking at re-entrant corners or over service trenches.</li></ol>",
  "/roof-framing-calculator": "<h2>How the roof goes up, step by step</h2><p>This page includes a step by step animated build sequence. The same steps are listed here so the method is readable without JavaScript. Bracing is shown before sheeting, because bracing is what holds the trusses upright.</p><ol><li><strong>Fix the wall plate</strong>: Bed and strap the wall plate level around the top of the walls. Every truss foot bears on it, so it carries the whole roof. Check first: Level within 10 mm all round, strapped to the blockwork.</li><li><strong>Erect the trusses</strong>: Stand each truss on the plate at the spacing on the drawing, prop and plumb it, then brace it before the next one goes up. Check first: Spacing even, trusses plumb, temporary bracing in place.</li><li><strong>Fix the purlins</strong>: Run purlins across the trusses at the gauge the sheeting needs, so a sheet always lands on a purlin at both edges. Check first: Gauge consistent to the ridge, purlins straight and fixed at every truss.</li><li><strong>Brace the roof plane</strong>: Fit diagonal and longitudinal bracing so the roof acts as one stiff plane under wind. This is what keeps the trusses upright. Check first: Both slopes braced, fixings through the truss, not just the purlin.</li><li><strong>Sheet and finish</strong>: Lay sheets from the leeward eave up to the ridge with the specified side lap, then fit ridge capping, flashing and gutters. Check first: Laps consistent, fixings on the crest or pan as specified, ridge sealed.</li></ol>",
  "/masonry-calculator": "<h2>How a block wall goes up, step by step</h2><p>This page includes a step by step animated build sequence. The same steps are listed here so the method is readable without JavaScript. Each course is staggered against the one below, so vertical joints never line up.</p><ol><li><strong>Set out the wall and the first course</strong>: Mark the wall line, set the corners to a square, then lay the first course dry to check the bond before any mortar is mixed. Check first: Diagonals equal and the bond closes without a cut at the corner.</li><li><strong>Build off a level foundation</strong>: The first course beds onto a foundation that is level and cured, because every course above it inherits any error in that line. Check first: Foundation level within 10 mm over the wall length.</li><li><strong>Bed each course on a full mortar joint</strong>: Spread a full bed joint, never a furrowed or spot bed, so the load is carried evenly along the wall rather than at points. Check first: Joint thickness consistent and fully filled.</li><li><strong>Build up course by course, staggered</strong>: Run each course staggered against the one below so vertical joints never line up, which is what gives a block wall its strength. Check first: No continuous vertical joint over the wall height.</li><li><strong>Form openings and add reinforcement</strong>: Hold window and door openings to width with a level, and place bed-joint reinforcement or lintels where the opening weakens the wall. Check first: Openings plumb and lintels bearing at both ends.</li><li><strong>Protect and cure the wall</strong>: Keep the wall damp and shaded for at least three days and keep scaffolding loads off the green mortar. Check first: No cracking along bed joints or at corners.</li></ol>",
  "/paving-calculator": "<h2>How paving is laid, layer by layer</h2><p>This page includes a step by step animated build sequence. The same steps are listed here so the method is readable without JavaScript. The bedding layer levels the pavers; it is never used to correct a bad sub-base.</p><ol><li><strong>Strip and compact the subgrade</strong>: Remove topsoil and soft spots, then compact the formation until it is firm and falls away from the building. Check first: No soft spots and water runs off, not into, the paving.</li><li><strong>Lay and compact the sub-base</strong>: Spread the sub-base in layers no deeper than 100 mm and compact each layer before the next goes down. Check first: Level within 10 mm over a 3 m straight edge.</li><li><strong>Screed the bedding sand</strong>: Lay a screeded bedding layer at a consistent thickness so every paver beds down evenly rather than rocking. Check first: Bedding never used to correct a bad sub-base.</li><li><strong>Lay the pavers</strong>: Lay from a fixed string line and work off the laid course, keeping joints even and cutting against edges. Check first: Joints even, bond followed, cuts at edges only.</li><li><strong>Brush in jointing sand and compact</strong>: Brush dry jointing sand into the joints, then plate compact so the sand is drawn in and the pavers interlock. Check first: Joints full, surface flush, no lipping between pavers.</li></ol>",
